<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What are conveyor guide rails?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Conveyor guide rails, also called conveyor rails, side rails, or side guides, are side-mounted rails that keep products centered on the conveyor belt or <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller surface</a>, prevent products from falling off edges, create lanes for multi-row product flow, and orient products for downstream operations. Guide rails are typically made from UHMW polyethylene, stainless steel, or aluminum with UHMW contact strips. Adjustable guide rails allow quick changeover between product sizes.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">What is the best material for conveyor guide rails?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>UHMW polyethylene is the most common guide rail material because it offers very low friction, good wear resistance, FDA compliance for food contact, and low cost. For heavy-duty applications or <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary washdown environments</a>, stainless steel rails with UHMW wear strips combine durability with low friction. Acetal (Delrin) provides higher rigidity for precision positioning applications.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">How do I adjust conveyor guide rails for different product sizes?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Adjustable guide rail systems use one of three methods: hand-knob clamps that slide on cross-bars and lock in position, hand-wheel or crank adjusters that move both rails simultaneously via threaded rods, or quick-release pin systems for predetermined positions. For frequent changeovers, choose systems with visual scales or detent positions. Motorized adjustment is available for automated changeover on high-volume <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How wide should the gap be between conveyor guide rails?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For standard transport, set the gap at 1/8" to 1/4" (3-6mm) wider than the product on each side. For precision positioning before labelers or vision systems, tighten to 1/16" to 1/8" (1.5-3mm) per side. For <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a>, widen to 1/4" to 1/2" (6-12mm) per side to prevent jamming under back-pressure.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">What are lane dividers on a conveyor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Lane dividers are internal guide rails that split a wide conveyor into multiple parallel lanes. They are used to increase throughput by running multiple product streams on a single conveyor, to separate different products or SKUs running simultaneously, and to create staging lanes before <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packing</a> or <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">palletizing</a>. Lane dividers are typically adjustable to accommodate different lane widths.</p>

</div>
